1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,177
Your CTO asks: "Why does every laptop
in the team fail with cannot lock ref

2
00:00:09,217 --> 00:00:12,134
'refs/heads/feature/login'
since this morning?"

3
00:00:12,474 --> 00:00:17,632
Nobody changed a setting. Nobody upgraded
Git. One person pushed one branch with a

4
00:00:17,672 --> 00:00:22,640
perfectly reasonable name, and since then
git fetch, the command that downloads

5
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:27,331
what's new from the server, fails on
every clone. What's your first guess?

6
00:00:27,371 --> 00:00:28,345
Say it out loud.

7
00:00:33,185 --> 00:00:38,260
Somebody once created a branch named feature.
And a ref, Git's word for a name that

8
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:42,326
points at a commit, can't be both
a name and a directory of names.

9
00:00:42,366 --> 00:00:47,256
That one rule about how refs are named
explains the incident, and the fix is one

10
00:00:47,296 --> 00:00:51,938
command. This video is about refs as
names: tags that don't move, names that

11
00:00:51,978 --> 00:00:56,806
collide, and names that nobody needs any
more. Keep that error message in mind.

12
00:00:56,846 --> 00:00:59,209
You'll produce it yourself in the demo.

13
00:01:01,049 --> 00:01:05,001
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:05,041 --> 00:01:08,862
This is the last video on refs,
and it's the practical one.

15
00:01:08,902 --> 00:01:09,648
Four topics.

16
00:01:09,988 --> 00:01:13,692
Lightweight tags: a ref that
doesn't move when you commit.

17
00:01:13,732 --> 00:01:18,728
Naming: what Git allows, what teams agree
on, and the feature versus feature/x

18
00:01:18,768 --> 00:01:23,957
conflict. Stale branches: how to answer
"which of our 340 branches can be deleted

19
00:01:23,997 --> 00:01:29,251
without losing anything". And the wrong-branch
commit from video 22, repaired with

20
00:01:29,291 --> 00:01:30,220
two ref writes.

21
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:33,638
Here's what you'll walk away with.

22
00:01:33,978 --> 00:01:35,390
After this video you can:

23
00:01:35,730 --> 00:01:39,495
Show that a lightweight tag is a ref
that does not move when you commit.

24
00:01:39,835 --> 00:01:44,634
Explain why feature and feature/x cannot
both exist, and what breaks for teammates.

25
00:01:44,974 --> 00:01:49,389
Find branches that are merged, gone or
old, and decide which may be deleted.

26
00:01:49,729 --> 00:01:53,985
Recognize a commit made on the wrong branch
as two ref writes, and plan the repair.

27
00:01:55,825 --> 00:02:01,591
Lightweight tags. In one sentence: a
lightweight tag is a ref under refs/tags/ that

28
00:02:01,631 --> 00:02:06,348
names an object directly. It's a
label that is not expected to move.

29
00:02:06,388 --> 00:02:12,014
git tag <name> is 🟢 SAFE: it adds a ref.
On screen, the tag v0.1.0 and the branch

30
00:02:12,054 --> 00:02:13,833
main name the same commit.

31
00:02:14,173 --> 00:02:19,309
An annotated tag, made with -a, is different:
the ref names a tag object with its

32
00:02:19,349 --> 00:02:24,548
own ID, tagger, date and message. The manual
reserves annotated tags for releases,

33
00:02:24,588 --> 00:02:28,510
and lightweight tags for "private
or temporary object labels".

34
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:34,585
Tags have no reflog by default. A reflog
is Git's local journal of where a name has

35
00:02:34,625 --> 00:02:40,152
pointed, and core.logAllRefUpdates creates
reflogs for branches, remote-tracking

36
00:02:40,192 --> 00:02:45,440
branches, notes and HEAD, not for tags.
So git tag -f and git tag -d, both 🟡

37
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:49,615
CAUTION, leave no trace except
the "was" ID in their output.

38
00:02:49,655 --> 00:02:50,590
Write it down.

39
00:02:50,930 --> 00:02:55,246
A branch and a tag with the same
short name are legal and confusing.

40
00:02:55,286 --> 00:03:00,244
Git resolves a short name by trying
refs/tags/<name> before refs/heads/<name>.

41
00:03:00,584 --> 00:03:06,504
Naming: conventions and rules. A branch name
is a path under refs/heads/, so slashes

42
00:03:06,544 --> 00:03:11,470
group branches: feature/retry-backoff,
fix/judge-timeout, release/0.2.

43
00:03:11,510 --> 00:03:16,365
Lowercase names with hyphens avoid
the case trap and need no quoting.

44
00:03:16,405 --> 00:03:17,926
Those are conventions.

45
00:03:18,266 --> 00:03:23,728
The rules are Git's. It rejects names with
a space, two consecutive dots, a tilde, a

46
00:03:23,768 --> 00:03:29,163
caret, a colon, a question mark, an asterisk,
an opening bracket, a backslash, or a

47
00:03:29,203 --> 00:03:34,665
control character. It rejects a component
that starts with a dot or ends with .lock,

48
00:03:34,705 --> 00:03:39,969
a trailing dot, the sequence @{, a leading
or trailing slash, and a double slash.

49
00:03:40,009 --> 00:03:45,209
And a branch name may not start with a
hyphen. No need to memorize the list: git

50
00:03:45,249 --> 00:03:50,251
check-ref-format --branch <name> tests
a candidate without creating anything.

51
00:03:50,591 --> 00:03:55,733
The conflict. One more rule follows from
the path structure: a name can't be both a

52
00:03:55,773 --> 00:04:00,727
ref and the prefix of other refs. With
the files backend, where a ref is a small

53
00:04:00,767 --> 00:04:04,597
file, the reason is visible:
refs/heads/feature is a file, and

54
00:04:04,637 --> 00:04:08,655
refs/heads/feature/login would
need a directory of the same name.

55
00:04:08,695 --> 00:04:13,524
But the rule is not a file-system accident.
It holds for packed refs, and in a

56
00:04:13,564 --> 00:04:16,708
reftable repository, where
there are no such files.

57
00:04:16,748 --> 00:04:21,452
Ref names form one hierarchy, and the
rule keeps every ref name unambiguous.

58
00:04:21,792 --> 00:04:27,064
The case trap. The default macOS volume
format doesn't distinguish main from MAIN.

59
00:04:27,104 --> 00:04:32,377
With the files backend a loose ref is a
file, so a wrong-case name finds the right

60
00:04:32,417 --> 00:04:37,430
file, and HEAD records the wrong name.
The textbook notes that the reasons for

61
00:04:37,470 --> 00:04:42,289
making reftable the default in Git 3.0
include exactly this class of clash.

62
00:04:42,629 --> 00:04:47,998
Stale branches. In one sentence: a stale
branch is a ref that nobody needs any more,

63
00:04:48,038 --> 00:04:53,214
and Git offers three tests for it, each
with a blind spot: age, reachability from

64
00:04:53,254 --> 00:04:55,081
main, and a deleted upstream.

65
00:04:55,421 --> 00:04:59,852
Age says when the branch last
changed, not whether its work landed.

66
00:04:59,892 --> 00:05:05,456
Reachability, git branch --merged main,
can't see a squash merge: main then contains

67
00:05:05,496 --> 00:05:09,793
a new commit with the same tree
and none of the branch's commits.

68
00:05:09,833 --> 00:05:14,931
A deleted upstream shows as [gone] in
git branch -vv after git fetch --prune.

69
00:05:14,971 --> 00:05:20,469
The upstream is the server branch your
branch follows, and that fetch is 🟡 CAUTION:

70
00:05:20,509 --> 00:05:25,139
it removes remote-tracking refs for
branches the server no longer has.

71
00:05:25,479 --> 00:05:30,168
On a team that deletes branches on the
server after merging, gone is the most

72
00:05:30,208 --> 00:05:35,266
reliable signal. Its blind spot: a branch
that was never pushed has no upstream and

73
00:05:35,306 --> 00:05:35,757
no gone.

74
00:05:36,097 --> 00:05:40,035
For a squash-merged branch there
is a proof that it's finished.

75
00:05:40,075 --> 00:05:45,087
git merge-tree --write-tree main <branch>
prints the tree a merge would produce.

76
00:05:45,127 --> 00:05:50,139
If it equals the tree of main, merging
the branch would change nothing, so -D is

77
00:05:50,179 --> 00:05:50,454
safe.

78
00:05:50,794 --> 00:05:54,438
Git 2.56 adds git branch
--delete-merged <pattern>.

79
00:05:54,478 --> 00:06:00,290
It deletes local branches whose configured
upstream matches the pattern, but only

80
00:06:00,330 --> 00:06:06,213
when their tip is reachable from that
upstream, with --dry-run to list them first.

81
00:06:06,253 --> 00:06:10,403
It's not run here: Git 2.55
reports the option as unknown.

82
00:06:10,443 --> 00:06:14,159
The textbook quotes two
limits from the 2.56 manual.

83
00:06:14,199 --> 00:06:20,083
It skips a branch when its configured
upstream ref no longer exists, so it doesn't

84
00:06:20,123 --> 00:06:25,212
handle [gone] branches. And a
squash-merged branch is silently skipped.

85
00:06:25,552 --> 00:06:30,484
What deleting does and does not do. Deleting
a local branch deletes a name in your

86
00:06:30,524 --> 00:06:35,395
clone. It doesn't delete the commits, and
it doesn't remove them from the server,

87
00:06:35,435 --> 00:06:40,428
from colleagues' clones or from a pull
request. A branch deletion is never a way to

88
00:06:40,468 --> 00:06:45,218
withdraw content. And a detail that
surprises people: a plain git branch -d can

89
00:06:45,258 --> 00:06:48,432
delete main. There is nothing
special about the name.

90
00:06:48,772 --> 00:06:53,481
Commits on the wrong branch. Because
branches are refs, the repair takes two ref

91
00:06:53,521 --> 00:06:58,290
writes and copies nothing. Here, two commits
sit on main that belong on a feature

92
00:06:58,330 --> 00:06:58,705
branch.

93
00:06:59,045 --> 00:07:04,431
First, git switch -c <feature> creates a
branch at the current commit and attaches

94
00:07:04,471 --> 00:07:09,460
HEAD to it. Then git branch -f main
origin/main, 🟡 CAUTION, moves main back.

95
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:13,231
The second is allowed once
main is no longer checked out.

96
00:07:15,071 --> 00:07:20,337
A picture helps. Think of refs as files in
folders, whatever the storage really is.

97
00:07:20,377 --> 00:07:25,643
refs/heads/feature is a file.
refs/heads/feature/login is a file in a folder called

98
00:07:25,683 --> 00:07:29,159
feature. One name can't be a
file and a folder at once.

99
00:07:29,499 --> 00:07:34,596
That model explains the conflict. It also
explains why a tag and a branch can share

100
00:07:34,636 --> 00:07:38,990
a short name: they're in different
folders, refs/tags/ and refs/heads/.

101
00:07:39,330 --> 00:07:44,136
Where the model breaks is instructive. The
storage is not always files: refs can be

102
00:07:44,176 --> 00:07:48,982
packed, or kept in reftable. The rule about
names holds anyway, because it's a rule

103
00:07:49,022 --> 00:07:53,477
about the hierarchy of names. And on a
case-insensitive file system the model

104
00:07:53,517 --> 00:07:58,322
becomes too literal. There, the folder can't
tell main from MAIN, and Git's idea of

105
00:07:58,362 --> 00:08:01,125
the name and the file
system's idea drift apart.

106
00:08:02,965 --> 00:08:06,911
Read the "Git state" line twice.
The conflict is not on the server.

107
00:08:06,951 --> 00:08:10,658
The server is consistent: it has
feature/login, and no feature.

108
00:08:10,698 --> 00:08:15,477
The conflict is in each clone, between a
stale remote-tracking ref and a new one.

109
00:08:17,317 --> 00:08:20,261
git tag v0.1.0 🟢 SAFE.

110
00:08:20,601 --> 00:08:26,092
The tag file holds the commit ID, and git
cat-file -t on the tag name reaches the

111
00:08:26,132 --> 00:08:29,574
commit: no object was
created. Now one more commit.

112
00:08:29,614 --> 00:08:33,192
Predict where the tag is
afterwards. Say it out loud.

113
00:08:38,032 --> 00:08:40,181
The branch moved, and the tag did not.

114
00:08:40,521 --> 00:08:46,700
The contrast: an annotated tag is an
object of type tag with its own ID, and

115
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:49,892
v0.2.0^{commit} peels it to the commit.

116
00:08:50,232 --> 00:08:54,231
.git/logs/refs has a heads
directory and no tags directory.

117
00:08:54,271 --> 00:08:59,776
git tag -f 🟡 CAUTION and git tag -d 🟡
CAUTION print the "was" ID, and that is all

118
00:08:59,816 --> 00:09:01,145
the record there is.

119
00:09:01,485 --> 00:09:07,345
Quick quiz. The next step creates a
branch named like the tag, v0.2.0.

120
00:09:07,385 --> 00:09:12,570
Which one does the short name
mean? A, the branch. B, the tag.

121
00:09:12,610 --> 00:09:14,930
C, Git refuses. Your answer?

122
00:09:19,770 --> 00:09:25,569
B. A branch named like the tag: the short
name means the tag, with a warning.

123
00:09:25,609 --> 00:09:31,863
heads/v0.2.0 and tags/v0.2.0 are unambiguous.
The script removes the branch with -D

124
00:09:31,903 --> 00:09:38,006
🔴 DANGEROUS. The branch pointed at a commit
that main reaches, so no commit loses

125
00:09:38,046 --> 00:09:39,067
its last name.

126
00:09:39,407 --> 00:09:44,179
A branch named feature exists. Predict
what git branch feature/login does.

127
00:09:44,219 --> 00:09:45,220
Say it out loud.

128
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:55,326
cannot lock ref: refs/heads/feature exists,
so refs/heads/feature/login can't be

129
00:09:55,366 --> 00:10:00,102
created. There it is: the message from
the hook, made with two commands.

130
00:10:00,442 --> 00:10:05,426
After git pack-refs --all there is no loose
file in the way, and the branch is still

131
00:10:05,466 --> 00:10:09,732
refused. And in a repository created
with the reftable format, the same.

132
00:10:10,072 --> 00:10:14,675
The fix is a rename, git branch -m 🟡
CAUTION. And the rule holds in the other

133
00:10:14,715 --> 00:10:18,656
direction: with feature/base in
place, a plain feature is refused.

134
00:10:18,996 --> 00:10:24,734
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper.
Four candidate names: fix bug, fix..bug,

135
00:10:24,774 --> 00:10:29,178
fix/judge.lock and fix/judge-timeout.
Which does Git accept?

136
00:10:29,218 --> 00:10:31,030
Say your answer out loud.

137
00:10:35,870 --> 00:10:40,952
Only the last one passes. A space, two
consecutive dots, and a component that ends

138
00:10:40,992 --> 00:10:42,889
with .lock are all on the list.

139
00:10:43,229 --> 00:10:48,349
Then a name that is legal and wrong. git
branch refs/heads/fix/judge-timeout created

140
00:10:48,389 --> 00:10:53,387
refs/heads/refs/heads/fix/judge-timeout,
because git branch takes a short name and

141
00:10:53,427 --> 00:10:55,107
prepends refs/heads/ itself.

142
00:10:57,947 --> 00:11:02,841
git switch MAIN succeeded. HEAD says
refs/heads/MAIN, and git branch lists main

143
00:11:02,881 --> 00:11:06,526
without an asterisk. The current
branch is not in the list.

144
00:11:06,866 --> 00:11:12,821
A commit in this state moves the file that
exists. git log --decorate prints (HEAD,

145
00:11:12,861 --> 00:11:15,204
main) rather than (HEAD -> main).

146
00:11:15,544 --> 00:11:19,793
Switching to the correctly spelled
name repairs it, and nothing was lost.

147
00:11:20,133 --> 00:11:25,065
Once the refs are packed, the file system
is out of the picture, and Main is created

148
00:11:25,105 --> 00:11:28,321
as a second ref. Two branches
that differ only in case.

149
00:11:31,161 --> 00:11:35,292
Test one, age: git for-each-ref
--sort=committerdate.

150
00:11:35,632 --> 00:11:40,210
Test two, reachability. --merged main
lists fix/empty-gold, merged with a merge

151
00:11:40,250 --> 00:11:44,478
commit. feature/rouge is missing,
although its content is in main: it was

152
00:11:44,518 --> 00:11:45,297
squash-merged.

153
00:11:45,637 --> 00:11:51,359
Test three: git fetch --prune 🟡
CAUTION, then [gone] in git branch -vv.

154
00:11:51,399 --> 00:11:57,039
wip/prompt-tuning was never pushed,
so it has no upstream and no gone.

155
00:11:57,379 --> 00:12:02,156
Predict which of the two finished branches
git branch -d 🟡 CAUTION will delete.

156
00:12:02,196 --> 00:12:03,803
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

157
00:12:08,643 --> 00:12:12,747
It deletes the merge-commit branch
and refuses the squash-merged one.

158
00:12:12,787 --> 00:12:17,672
Its upstream is gone, so Git falls back to
HEAD, and finds commits that main can't

159
00:12:17,712 --> 00:12:22,477
reach. The git merge-tree comparison proves
that merging the branch would change

160
00:12:22,517 --> 00:12:22,957
nothing.

161
00:12:23,297 --> 00:12:27,865
And Git 2.55's answer to
the 2.56 option: unknown.

162
00:12:30,705 --> 00:12:36,143
Two commits on main that belong on a feature
branch, and a third, uncommitted edit.

163
00:12:36,183 --> 00:12:40,630
[ahead 2] says the commits are local
only, so the repair is private.

164
00:12:40,670 --> 00:12:44,458
Plan it before you see it:
which two refs must be written?

165
00:12:44,498 --> 00:12:45,645
Say them out loud.

166
00:12:50,485 --> 00:12:55,716
git switch -c feature/judge-cache 🟢 SAFE:
the index and working tree are untouched,

167
00:12:55,756 --> 00:13:00,987
so the uncommitted edit comes along. git
branch -f main origin/main 🟡 CAUTION moves

168
00:13:01,027 --> 00:13:04,416
main back to the published
commit. Nothing was copied.

169
00:13:04,756 --> 00:13:06,683
The reflogs record both writes.

170
00:13:08,523 --> 00:13:10,171
Five mistakes to watch for.

171
00:13:10,511 --> 00:13:15,974
cannot lock ref 'refs/heads/feature/x'.
Root cause: a branch named feature exists,

172
00:13:16,014 --> 00:13:19,195
and a name cannot be a
ref and a prefix of refs.

173
00:13:19,535 --> 00:13:24,480
git fetch fails with "unable to update
local ref". Root cause: a stale

174
00:13:24,520 --> 00:13:30,176
origin/feature in the clone blocks the
new origin/feature/...; git fetch --prune

175
00:13:30,216 --> 00:13:30,959
removes it.

176
00:13:31,299 --> 00:13:34,715
git branch -d refuses although
the branch "was merged".

177
00:13:34,755 --> 00:13:39,677
Root cause: a squash merge put the content,
not the commits, into main, and the

178
00:13:39,717 --> 00:13:42,379
upstream is gone, so
-d tests against HEAD.

179
00:13:42,719 --> 00:13:47,913
git branch shows no asterisk. Root cause:
a wrong-case name was accepted on a

180
00:13:47,953 --> 00:13:53,215
case-insensitive file system, and HEAD
records that name; check cat .git/HEAD.

181
00:13:53,555 --> 00:13:58,208
Deleting a branch to withdraw content.
Root cause: deletion removes a name, not

182
00:13:58,248 --> 00:14:02,545
commits, and nothing on the server,
in other clones or in a pull request.

183
00:14:04,385 --> 00:14:09,953
Now, out of the lab, to the CTO's 340
branches. Deleting the branch on the server is

184
00:14:09,993 --> 00:14:15,360
a push, and affects everyone's next prune.
Before that, the textbook's procedure:

185
00:14:15,400 --> 00:14:20,900
apply the three tests, and for each candidate
run git log --oneline main..<branch>.

186
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:26,241
If that range is empty, or every commit
in it is squash-merged, nothing is lost.

187
00:14:26,581 --> 00:14:31,959
A team that deletes branches on the server
in the same step as the merge keeps the

188
00:14:31,999 --> 00:14:35,593
list short, and gets [gone]
as a reliable local signal.

189
00:14:35,633 --> 00:14:41,143
And the textbook's prevention for the
morning's incident: fetch.prune=true on a team

190
00:14:41,183 --> 00:14:45,503
that uses namespaces, and top-level
names reserved for namespaces.

191
00:14:47,343 --> 00:14:52,995
Your turn. Do Lab 4.3, "The feature
versus feature/x conflict", in

192
00:14:53,035 --> 00:14:56,186
lab-manual/m04-refs-branches-head.md.

193
00:14:56,526 --> 00:15:01,188
The lab runs the incident from both sides.
Before the fetch that fails, list the

194
00:15:01,228 --> 00:15:06,008
refs in the clone and on the server, and
predict the exact ref that the fetch will

195
00:15:06,048 --> 00:15:07,301
not be able to create.

196
00:15:09,141 --> 00:15:14,668
Q109: "Why does git fetch fail on every
clone after someone pushes feature/login,

197
00:15:14,708 --> 00:15:16,935
and what is the one-command fix?"

198
00:15:19,275 --> 00:15:24,139
Answer out loud. A strong answer places the
conflict in the right repository, names

199
00:15:24,179 --> 00:15:29,044
the two refs involved and the rule they
break, and says why the rule is not a quirk

200
00:15:29,084 --> 00:15:32,352
of files on disk. Give the
fix, and then the prevention.

201
00:15:34,192 --> 00:15:39,407
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: a lightweight tag is a ref under

202
00:15:39,447 --> 00:15:43,808
refs/tags/ that points at a commit
and does not move when I commit.

203
00:15:43,848 --> 00:15:48,800
Tags have no reflog by default. Ref
names form one hierarchy, so feature and

204
00:15:48,840 --> 00:15:51,887
feature/x can't both
exist, in any ref storage.

205
00:15:52,227 --> 00:15:57,015
A stale origin/feature breaks fetch until
I prune. On a case-insensitive file

206
00:15:57,055 --> 00:16:01,405
system, a wrong-case branch name can
be accepted and recorded in HEAD.

207
00:16:01,445 --> 00:16:06,546
Three tests find stale branches: age,
reachability from main, and a gone upstream.

208
00:16:06,586 --> 00:16:11,625
A squash merge defeats reachability. And
a commit on the wrong branch is repaired

209
00:16:11,665 --> 00:16:12,879
with two ref writes.

210
00:16:14,719 --> 00:16:21,991
Read sections 7.11 to 7.16 of Chapter
7, and do the Practice section 7.18.

211
00:16:22,331 --> 00:16:28,681
Do Exercise 4.3, Level 1, "a lightweight
tag is a ref that does not move", and

212
00:16:28,721 --> 00:16:34,743
Exercise 4.8, Level 3, "the deploy
script that picks the wrong commit", in

213
00:16:34,783 --> 00:16:37,446
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

214
00:16:37,786 --> 00:16:43,373
Challenge: Exercise 4.9, Level 4, "three
commits on the wrong branch", in the same

215
00:16:43,413 --> 00:16:43,716
file.

216
00:16:44,056 --> 00:16:49,014
You can now read an error about names as
a statement about one hierarchy, and that

217
00:16:49,054 --> 00:16:54,073
calms a whole team on a bad morning. Do the
lab: making the failure yourself is the

218
00:16:54,113 --> 00:16:58,889
quickest way to stop fearing it. Next
time: configuration, with its scopes, its

219
00:16:58,929 --> 00:17:03,948
precedence, and how to read and write
settings. Until then, look at the state first

220
00:17:03,988 --> 00:17:06,447
and type second. See you in the next one.
